    {
      "filename": "PNG Kapkap disc-QM-r.jpg",
      "title": "File:PNG Kapkap disc-QM-r.jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4c/PNG_Kapkap_disc-QM-r.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PNG_Kapkap_disc-QM-r.jpg",
      "width": 2560,
      "height": 1920,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0",
      "artist": "Queensland Museum",
      "description": "Kapkaps are made from clam shell and turtle shell. They are like ornaments that can be worn as a pendant around the neck, or on a headband on the forehead or side of the head. \nObject description: \nThis kapkap is from New Ireland Province in Papua New Guinea. It is made from a section of clam shell that has been ground down to form a flat disc. An intricate design has been carved out of turtle shell and affixed to the clam shell disc.    \nHistory: \n\nKapkaps are only worn on special occasions, like weddings, feasts and funerals. Only people who have earned the right to be called ‘maimais’, can wear kapkaps. A maimai is a family or clan leader, and they know a lot about traditional culture and customs. In New Ireland, only men can become maimais. The type of designs on a kapkap can show people what ranking, or type of knowledge the maimai has. When the maimai learns new knowledge, or earns the right to speak for another family or clan, a new kapkap is made for him. It can take months to make a new kapkap.",
      "dateCreated": "2009-09-22",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "new-ireland-province-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "rr7w",
      "mediumPath": "_m/r/r/7/w/new-ireland-province-wp/png-kapkap-disc-qm-r-medium.webp",
      "smallPath": "_m/r/r/7/w/new-ireland-province-wp/png-kapkap-disc-qm-r-small.webp"
    },
    {
      "filename": "PNG Pipes QM r.jpg",
      "title": "File:PNG Pipes QM r.jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/af/PNG_Pipes_QM_r.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PNG_Pipes_QM_r.jpg",
      "width": 2560,
      "height": 1920,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0",
      "artist": "Queensland Museum",
      "description": "Wooden pipes made of thin bamboo are used for music making and ceremonies. Similar in design to large organ pipes, the length of each section affects the pitch and resonance of the sound it produces. This instrument was made in New Ireland province - an offshore island of Papua New Guinea.",
      "dateCreated": "2009-09-22",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "new-ireland-province-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "rr7w",
